# Bug Triage Workflow

Use this workflow when a crash, error, support report, QA bug, or analytics anomaly appears.

```text
Raw signal
  ↓
Crash Analytics / Support Feedback / QA Agent
  ↓
Bug Reproduction Agent
  ↓
Triage Master
  ↓
Ticket creation
  ↓
Orchestrator routes to owner
  ↓
Implementation
  ↓
Regression tests
  ↓
Release gate or hotfix path
  ↓
Monitoring confirms fix
```

Bug ticket must include:

- Title
- Severity
- Priority
- Affected version
- Affected environment
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected behavior
- Actual behavior
- Logs/crash traces/screenshots
- Suspected cause
- Acceptance criteria
- Regression test requirement

---

# Hotfix Workflow

Use this when production impact is high.

```text
Incident detected
  ↓
Incident Commander classifies severity
  ↓
Crash Analytics identifies scope
  ↓
Release Engineer evaluates rollback
  ↓
Orchestrator chooses rollback or hotfix
  ↓
Developer implements minimal safe fix
  ↓
Code Reviewer + Test Critic + Security Critic review
  ↓
Release Gatekeeper approves emergency release
  ↓
Deploy
  ↓
Observability confirms recovery
  ↓
Postmortem + follow-up tickets
```

Hotfix rules:

- Prefer rollback if faster and safer.
- Keep code changes minimal.
- Add regression tests after stabilization if not possible during the emergency.
- Always write a postmortem for severe incidents.

---

# Definition of Done

A feature is not done until:

- Requirements are satisfied.
- Acceptance criteria pass.
- UI states are implemented.
- Accessibility is checked.
- Unit/component/integration/E2E tests are added as appropriate.
- Security and privacy implications are reviewed.
- Performance impact is acceptable.
- Documentation is updated.
- Observability is added if the feature is operationally important.
- Release notes or changelog entries are prepared when user-visible.

